1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:14,483
A conditional branch compares two registers, rs1 and rs2, and also needs a jump target, and since it writes no register,

2
00:00:14,483 --> 00:00:15,381
it has no rd.

3
00:00:15,381 --> 00:00:23,043
But the target address itself is thirty-two bits, which can't possibly fit inside a thirty-two bit instruction.

4
00:00:23,043 --> 00:00:24,600
So what do we do?

5
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:27,906
Notice that branches usually jump only a short way,

6
00:00:27,906 --> 00:00:32,704
since an if or a loop body is typically a few to a few dozen instructions.

7
00:00:32,704 --> 00:00:39,300
So we encode only the offset relative to the current PC, which is called PC-relative addressing.

8
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:46,299
RISC-V leaves room for sixteen-bit compressed instructions, so instruction addresses are always multiples of two,

9
00:00:46,299 --> 00:00:51,131
and the lowest bit of the offset is always zero, which we don't need to store.

10
00:00:51,131 --> 00:00:57,325
So twelve bits can represent a thirteen-bit offset, with a range of about four kibibytes either way,

11
00:00:57,325 --> 00:01:00,050
or roughly a thousand instructions each way.

12
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:05,811
There is another benefit, which is that if the whole block of code moves elsewhere in memory,

13
00:01:05,811 --> 00:01:08,351
the branch offsets need no change at all.

14
00:01:08,351 --> 00:01:12,066
This is called position-independent code.

15
00:01:12,066 --> 00:01:15,419
The B format is laid out almost like the S format.

16
00:01:15,419 --> 00:01:22,661
The fields rs1, rs2, funct3, and opcode are in their old places, and the immediate is split into two pieces.

17
00:01:22,661 --> 00:01:29,567
But the order of the immediate's bits is scrambled, with bit twelve at the very top of the instruction,

18
00:01:29,567 --> 00:01:33,322
and bit eleven moved to the end of the right-hand piece.

19
00:01:33,322 --> 00:01:36,273
This is to reuse the wiring of the S format.

20
00:01:36,273 --> 00:01:45,700
Bits ten to five and bits four to one are in exactly the same places as in S, and the sign bit is always bit thirty-one.

21
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:48,064
Let's encode beq x19, x10, End.

22
00:01:48,064 --> 00:01:52,870
End is four instructions ahead, so the offset is sixteen bytes.

23
00:01:52,870 --> 00:02:01,262
Sixteen as a thirteen-bit binary number is zero, zero zero zero zero, zero zero zero one, zero zero zero zero.

24
00:02:01,262 --> 00:02:07,733
The lowest bit isn't stored, and the other bits go into their prescribed places.

25
00:02:07,733 --> 00:02:16,533
The registers are as usual, rs2 is x10 and rs1 is x19, the funct3 of beq is zero zero zero,

26
00:02:16,533 --> 00:02:21,949
and the branch opcode is one one zero zero zero one one.

27
00:02:21,949 --> 00:02:27,000
So the final machine code is 0x00A98863.

28
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,558
A twelve-bit immediate holds only small constants.

29
00:02:30,558 --> 00:02:36,039
So how do we get a thirty-two bit constant, like 0xDEADBEEF, into a register?

30
00:02:36,039 --> 00:02:43,797
The U format has just rd and a twenty-bit immediate, and the lui instruction, short for load upper immediate,

31
00:02:43,797 --> 00:02:45,366
uses this format.

32
00:02:45,366 --> 00:02:52,886
The instruction lui t0, 0xDEADB puts the twenty-bit immediate into the upper twenty bits of t0,

33
00:02:52,886 --> 00:02:57,500
and clears the lower twelve bits to zero.

34
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:01,750
Can we then add the low twelve bits, 0xEEF, with an addi?

35
00:03:01,750 --> 00:03:05,404
Careful, because the immediate of addi is signed.

36
00:03:05,404 --> 00:03:13,979
The top bit of 0xEEF is a one, so as a twelve-bit two's complement number it means minus two hundred seventy-three,

37
00:03:13,979 --> 00:03:17,333
and it gets sign-extended to 0xFFFFFEEF.

38
00:03:17,333 --> 00:03:23,995
The sum comes out as 0xDEADAEEF, so one has been borrowed from the upper twenty bits.

39
00:03:23,995 --> 00:03:33,401
The fix is, when the top bit of the low twelve bits is one, to add one to the upper twenty bits first, and write lui t0,

40
00:03:33,401 --> 00:03:34,400
0xDEADC.

41
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:40,293
Luckily we don't need to work this out by hand, because the pseudo-instruction li t0,

42
00:03:40,293 --> 00:03:45,008
0xDEADBEEF is expanded by the assembler into these two instructions.

43
00:03:45,008 --> 00:03:52,497
Another U-format instruction is auipc, which shifts the immediate left by twelve bits and adds it to the PC,

44
00:03:52,497 --> 00:03:57,333
to work out an address far from the current position.

45
00:03:57,333 --> 00:04:04,928
The jal instruction needs only rd and an offset, so the J format gives all the remaining twenty bits to the immediate.

46
00:04:04,928 --> 00:04:09,112
As before, the unit is two bytes and the lowest bit isn't stored.

47
00:04:09,112 --> 00:04:16,642
So the offset is twenty-one bits, with a range of about one mebibyte either way, enough to cover most function calls.

48
00:04:16,642 --> 00:04:23,723
The bit order is scrambled again, so that as many bits as possible line up with the I format and the U format,

49
00:04:23,723 --> 00:04:27,100
and the sign bit still stays at bit thirty-one.

50
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:29,541
What if the target is farther away?

51
00:04:29,541 --> 00:04:37,075
The jalr instruction is an I format, which jumps to rs1 plus the immediate, and stores PC plus four into rd.

52
00:04:37,075 --> 00:04:45,167
Together with auipc computing the upper twenty bits first, we can jump anywhere in the thirty-two bit address space,

53
00:04:45,167 --> 00:04:50,233
and that is how the pseudo-instruction call is expanded.

54
00:04:50,233 --> 00:04:53,449
Now let's put all six RV32I formats together.

55
00:04:53,449 --> 00:05:01,238
The positions of rs1, rs2, rd, and the opcode never change, and only the arrangement of the immediate varies.

56
00:05:01,238 --> 00:05:05,454
And the sign bit of the immediate is always bit thirty-one,

57
00:05:05,454 --> 00:05:12,566
so the hardware can start sign extension before it even knows the instruction type.

